# nvn/pso: build only the head of the prewarm lists before the first frame, the tail in the background (DM_PSO_PREPARE_PERCENT)
#
# Origin:  written in this repository for the owner's decision "compile half of the prewarm lists before the first frame" (Ocarina of Time, Switch 2, 0400486024DEC000). No upstream
#          commit: nvn_mtl.m's prepare step (warm_start -> warm_prepare) builds EVERY line of the lists before the first frame, on hw.activecpu workers (about 25k libraries and pipelines
#          for this game on a first start). Applies after 0411 (patch -p1 --forward, no fuzz, no offset), to the tree out-stage/out-engine/engine + engine-patches/0401..0411.
# Licence: the licence of the tree it patches (the engine's MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2026 seedds and contributors).
#
# WHAT IT ADDS
#   DM_PSO_PREPARE_PERCENT=<1..100>   default 100 (unset, empty, or anything that is not a whole number from 1 to 100: ignored with one log line, i.e. 100).
#       With p < 100 the prepare step builds the first ceil(n * p / 100) of the lists' n TASKS (the lists' order: the profile's pso_manifest.tsv, then this device's pso_user.tsv; a render
#       task is the pipelines of one shader pair, so lines >= tasks) before the first frame, with the progress screen counting the head's lines, and does NOT build the rest (the tail) there.
#   DM_PSO_PREPARE_TAIL=0             default on. On: the tail is built after the prepare step (the pipelines' prebuild included) by ONE serial worker at QOS_CLASS_UTILITY while the
#       game runs. Off: the tail is not built at all, and a draw that needs one of its pipelines builds it on demand, as for any pipeline the lists do not hold.
#   A draw that needs a tail pipeline before the worker reached it takes today's on-demand path (the app sets DM_PSO_NOWAIT=0: the draw waits for its compile). warm_claim keeps the first
#   pipeline in the cache and drops the later duplicate, as it already did for the prefetch and the background prewarm.
#   New: dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_warm_head.h (pure C: warm_head_count, warm_percent_parse, warm_claim_index), tests dm/gfx/tests/t_warm_head.c (no GPU) and t_pso_head.m (the real warm_start on the
#   Mac's GPU with synthetic shaders), Makefile rules for both.
#   Log lines (MLOG): "pipeline prepare: DM_PSO_PREPARE_PERCENT=p: head H lines (the first k of n tasks) before the first frame, tail T lines (m tasks) afterwards in the background |
#   SKIPPED (DM_PSO_PREPARE_TAIL=0)...", "pipeline prepare: head D of H lines done in S s", "pipeline prewarm head done: ...", "pipeline prewarm tail: started ... / N of M tasks done (each
#   60 s) / finished, in S s / stopped after N of M tasks", then the usual "pipeline prewarm done: ..." at the tail's end. With p = 100 none of these is printed.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR (read the diff): prepare_percent() is 100 -> warm_head_count(n, 100) == n -> head == n, head_lines == lines, split == 0, tail == 0 -> warm_prepare gets
#   head == tasks.count (its loop takes the same indexes: warm_claim_index(&next, n) is atomic_fetch_add + "i >= n" as it was), warm_finish_ex(1, 0) is the old warm_finish (same sweep,
#   same text "pipeline prewarm done: ...", dictionary released, running cleared, arch_flush, pso_prebuild_bg; warm_finish() is kept as that call, t_pso_warm calls it), then
#   prepare_prebuild() as before, warm_tail_start() is not reached. The background branch (DM_PSO_PREPARE=0, no window) is not touched. t_pso_head runs this path (unset, 100, 99 of 40
#   tasks, invalid values) and checks lines, libraries, pipelines, dictionary, sweep, log; the Mac's t_pso_warm (123 checks, both compilers) still passes.
#
# CAREFUL POINT (a): WHAT RUNS AFTER THE HEAD AND WHAT ONLY AFTER THE TAIL
#   After the head (warm_finish_ex(0, tail)): the log, g_warm_running = 0, arch_flush() (the head's captured bases are written now, as before the first frame today), NOT the archive
#     sweep, and the shader dictionary and load table (g_warm_dict / g_warm_loads) are KEPT when a tail follows, released when none does. Then prepare_prebuild(), exactly as today, with
#     g_warm_running = 0 (it relies on arch_flush() handing the period over and on the prebuilt.tsv stamp it writes afterwards: with the flag still 1 both would be held back).
#   Then, only if a tail follows, warm_tail_start(): g_warm_bg = 1 and g_warm_running = 1 again (for the tail the prewarm is what it is in the app without a prepare step: running beside
#     the game; the archive's timer flush then follows arch_warm_flush_due, 60 s then 120 s, ..., and not one file every 10 s), and one block on bg_group at utility QoS.
#   Only after the tail (warm_finish, on the tail's thread): g_sweep_warm = 1 and arch_sweep(), the "done" line, release of the dictionary, g_warm_running = 0, arch_flush().
#   Why the sweep waits: arch_sweep removes the archive files none of whose pipelines a lookup took in this run, on the premise that the lists asked for every pipeline (g_sweep_warm).
#   After a head alone that is false: a file that holds only tail pipelines would go and be compiled again. So the sweep runs when every line was asked for (p = 100, or at the tail's end)
#   and never with DM_PSO_PREPARE_TAIL=0 or after a stop. A start that compacts the archive (g_compact_enabled, more than 8 files in a tier) ignores the percentage and builds everything
#   before the first frame: compaction recaptures what the prewarm finds in the old files and closes (deleting them) at the prewarm's end, so it has to see the whole prewarm, and the
#   head's end, with g_warm_running = 0, would close it early (arch_hand_over).
#   Threads: the head's workers (user-initiated QoS, all joined when warm_prepare returns: dispatch_group_wait) call warm_task concurrently as today: g_warm_dict / g_warm_loads under
#   g_warm_dict_lock (never held across a library load: warm_shader waits on the load's dispatch group outside it), g_warm_items under g_warm_lock, g_warm_shaders / rps / cps / mps /
#   skip_* / done atomics. From the head's end on ONE thread calls warm_task, the tail's worker (serial queue, its block starts after the head's workers joined and after the prebuild),
#   and warm_shader is reached only from warm_task (grep: no other caller): the tail is the only user of g_warm_dict and g_warm_loads, so nothing races with them and nothing can use
#   them after release. They are released by exactly one of: the tail's own end or stop branch (its last act, on its thread), the head's end when no tail follows (no tail thread exists),
#   or dm_gfx_teardown AFTER its dispatch_group_wait(bg_group()), which has joined the tail (bg_async puts the tail on bg_group; teardown sets g_gfx_teardown first, a block not yet
#   started returns at once, one under way ends at its next loop test). The game's own builds do not touch them (shader_get, pso_get_, the prefetch: other paths). The tail's results
#   reach the game only through warm_enqueue / warm_claim (g_warm_lock; g_warm_ready release/acquire), exactly as the background prewarm's do. The one object a tail task and a draw can build
#   at once, the same library or pipeline, is built twice at worst: warm_claim keeps the one already in g_shcache / g_pso.
#   Verified on the Mac (t_pso_head): ThreadSanitizer 0 reports and AddressSanitizer 0 errors in both compiler modes, with the game's thread claiming and loading on demand beside the tail.
# CAREFUL POINT (b): PROGRESS. warm_prepare takes the head's task count and the head's lines (the sum of the head tasks' lines): front_progress and the "pipeline prepare:" lines count
#   g_warm_done (lines done) against the head's lines, so the screen reaches 100 % at the head's end. g_warm_done goes on counting in the tail (mtl_warm_stop's message prints it).
# CAREFUL POINT (c): STOP, TEARDOWN, BACKGROUND. The tail honours g_gfx_teardown and g_warm_stop between two tasks (a render task also tests g_warm_stop between its pipelines, as today);
#   either ends it without warm_finish ("stopped after N of M tasks", dictionary released, running cleared, no sweep): what is left is built at the next start or on demand. A start that
#   finds the process or the graphics ending does not start the tail. dm_gfx_teardown waits for the task under way, no more. The head's own teardown handling is as it was.
#   Going to the background (dm_gfx_suspend): not hooked, as for the background prewarm: the tail goes on until iOS suspends the process, and the archive writes the period under way
#   (mtl_arch_save any_time) each time, which adds a small file per background while the tail runs. Pipeline compiles are not GPU command buffers, but nothing here was run on a phone.
# CAREFUL POINT (d): prepare_prebuild (the pulling base of every pair of shader_pairs.tsv and pso_pairs.tsv, before the first frame, "Preparing pipelines", once per chain / depth mode /
#   pair list: prebuilt.tsv) is LEFT AS IT IS and still builds every pair before the first frame whatever the percentage. It is also large for this game: pso_pairs.tsv has 6,472 rows and
#   the engine tree's profile also has shader_pairs.tsv (28,179 lines; the prebuild reads both): the engine's own note measured 34,649 pairs, 493 s on an M4 Pro with 14 workers for
#   the prebuild tool. So the head setting shortens only the lists' part of the first Play; on a cold start the prebuild may then be most of what is left (DM_PSO_PREBUILD=0, an existing
#   switch, skips it and leaves the bases to the draws). It may also make the tail cheaper (the pulling bases of the tail's render pairs are then already built or archived): not measured.
#
# MEASURED on this Mac (M1 Pro): t_warm_head 455,404 checks 0 failed (also under UBSan+integer and TSan); t_pso_head 348 checks 0 failed with DM_PSO_MTL4=0 and =1 (also under TSan and
#   ASan); a mutation (head end releasing the dictionary and allowing the sweep) makes 8 of its checks fail. NOT VERIFIED: anything on an iPhone (head or tail time, frame time while the
#   tail runs, memory, the progress screen, a background / foreground), the app's way of setting the two variables, the pack's own manifest (14,753 rows). For the engine tree's profile
#   manifest of this game (25,163 rows: a Python emulation of warm_parse without its shader-map test finds 23,191 tasks, 16,818 S, 5,166 R pairs with 7,138 pipelines, 1,043 C, 164 M),
#   whose first fifth is almost only library loads (31 of its pipelines): at 50 % the head holds 51.9 % of the lines but 47.6 % of the render pipelines and 36.5 % of the compute ones.
# RISKS: head-by-file-order is only meaningful if the manifest was recorded chronologically (it interleaves S runs and R/C rows: consistent with a recording order, not proven);
#   background compiles cost frame time (the engine's own note: an iPhone 17 Pro Max's GPU frame went from about 13 ms to 15-18 ms in Breath of the Wild with background builds at
#   user-initiated QoS; one utility worker should cost less, not measured); a draw that reaches a tail pipeline early hitches once; a killed process loses the tail's captures not yet
#   written (the period under way, up to 60 s old at the start, then 120 s ...); many small archive files (a quit or a background during the tail writes one) can make a later start a
#   compaction, which builds everything before the first frame.
# Roll back: remove this file from engine-patches/ and run scripts/build-host.sh again (docs/ENGINE-UPDATE.md, section 10); the two variables do nothing without it.

